lems extremely well.\n\nTeamwork/communication: A leadership qualities but maybe not good at criticism. B uncooperative, grumpy. C creates positive atmosphere but difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric. D weak leadership, arrogant.\n\nProfessional development: B excellent computer skills (maybe ongoing). A maybe not. C not willing to further education (negative). D weak leadership.\n\nOverall, candidate C is reliable, good decision-making, positive atmosphere, but communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education. Those are significant for crew cooperation and long-term growth.\n\nCandidate B handles stress, conscientious, good weather assessment, but grumpy and uncooperative, which could affect crew.\n\nCandidate D strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but arrogant and weak leadership.\n\nCandidate A strong anticipation, spatial vision, leadership, but unorganized and poor at criticism.\n\nLong-distance flights require strong decision-making, reliability, ability to handle stress, good crew communication, and professional development.\n\nWhich candidate balances? Possibly Candidate A: leadership, anticipation, spatial vision important for navigation, but unorganized and poor at criticism could affect crew. However, leadership may offset.\n\nCandidate D: problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but arrogance and weak leadership may hinder crew.\n\nCandidate B: stress handling, weather assessment crucial for long flights, but uncooperative.\n\nCandidate C: reliability and quick decisions, positive atmosphere, but communication difficulty and egocentric may cause crew friction.\n\nWe need provisional preference. I think Candidate A seems strong on technical and leadership, but weaknesses may be mitigated? However we cannot mitigate. The rule says weigh weaknesses as evidence, not mitigate. So unorganized and poor at criticism are notable for long flights where organization and feedback are important.\n\nCandidate D's ar